From: degas device tree
Subject: [PATCH] SystemUI: composite the UDFPS overlay at the panel peak refresh rate

Unlocking with the fingerprint sensor brightens the WHOLE screen - brighter than
the manual maximum - whenever the display is running at 60, 90 or 120 Hz. At
144 Hz only the spot under the finger lights up, which is the intended LHBM
behaviour.

The panel and its driver are not at fault. Driving LHBM by hand through
/sys/class/mi_display/disp-DSI-0/disp_param ("12 1" then "9 1") produces the
spot and nothing else at every refresh rate, and dmesg for a real unlock is
identical at 60 and at 144: LHBM_ON_WHITE_1000NIT -> local_hbm value 1 ->
LOCAL_HBM_NORMAL_WHITE_1300NIT, bl_lvl=8191, fod_ui_ready notify=8. The global
hbm[01] feature is never switched on. So the flash is composition, not panel
state: the frames of the transition - sensor area driven to full brightness, the
white FOD circle, the rest of the screen dimmed - stay on screen long enough at
a low refresh rate to be seen as one bright frame, and at 144 Hz they do not.

Ask for the peak refresh rate on the overlay window itself, so the panel is
already there by the time the FOD circle is shown, rather than mode-setting
during authentication. DisplayContent.applySurfaceChangesTransaction() takes
preferredRefreshRate from the topmost displayed window with a surface and does
not require focus, so this counts even though the overlay is an unfocused
TYPE_NAVIGATION_BAR_PANEL with INPUT_FEATURE_SPY.

The rate comes from config_defaultPeakRefreshRate (144 on degas) rather than a
literal, and a device that leaves it at 0 gets preferredRefreshRate = 0, which
is the "no preference" default this window had before.

Cost: while the UDFPS overlay is up - which on the keyguard is the whole time
the lock screen is showing - the display is pinned to its peak rate instead of
idling down to 60 Hz. That is the trade this bug is worth; revisit if it shows
up in idle drain measurements.

Unrelated to the refresh-rate heal service: that one puts the DDIC back to 144
after the finger is lifted, this one fixes the brightness before and during.
diff --git a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/biometrics/UdfpsControllerOverlay.kt b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/biometrics/UdfpsControllerOverlay.kt
index a80a96dc5..a9194e660 100644
--- a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/biometrics/UdfpsControllerOverlay.kt
+++ b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/biometrics/UdfpsControllerOverlay.kt
@@ -131,6 +131,16 @@ constructor(
         null
     }
 
+    // degas: composite the UDFPS overlay at the panel peak refresh rate. Below 144 Hz the
+    // LHBM transition frames are on screen long enough to read as a full-screen brightness
+    // flash on unlock; at 144 Hz they are not. DisplayContent takes preferredRefreshRate
+    // from the topmost displayed window, so an unfocused overlay still counts. A 0 here
+    // (device without config_defaultPeakRefreshRate) means "no preference", as before.
+    private val peakRefreshRate =
+        context.resources
+            .getInteger(com.android.internal.R.integer.config_defaultPeakRefreshRate)
+            .toFloat()
+
     private val coreLayoutParams =
         WindowManager.LayoutParams(
                 WindowManager.LayoutParams.TYPE_NAVIGATION_BAR_PANEL,
@@ -150,6 +160,7 @@ constructor(
                 // Avoid announcing window title.
                 accessibilityTitle = " "
                 inputFeatures = WindowManager.LayoutParams.INPUT_FEATURE_SPY
+                preferredRefreshRate = peakRefreshRate
             }
 
     /** If the overlay is currently showing. */
